What was wrong
In humanize() auto mode, weeks/months used a fixed _SECS_PER_MONTH = 30.5 days and the month count was a bare year*12 + month index:
elif diff < self._SECS_PER_MONTH:          # 30.5-day bucket
    ...weeks...
elif diff < self._SECS_PER_MONTH * 2:      # "a month"
elif diff < self._SECS_PER_YEAR:           # count = year*12+month (day ignored)
Two inconsistencies, hence "inaccurate calibration":
- Bucket vs. real months: Jan 31 → Feb 28 is a true 1-month shift, but 28 days < 30.5, so it fell into the weeks bucket → "4 weeks ago".
- Calendar index vs. elapsed time: Feb 15 → Apr 15 = 59 days (exactly a 2-month shift) sat under the 61-day singular bound → "a month"; and Dec 31 2020 → Mar 15 2021 (74 d, really 2 months + 15 d) → "3 months".
The fix (arrow/arrow.py:1209)
Use dateutil.relativedelta — which counts completed calendar months day-of-month–aware, including end-of-month clamping (Jan 31 → Feb 28 = 1 month) — as the single source of truth for both the boundary and the count:
- completed_months computed from the earlier date to the later one (I found relativedelta is asymmetric at month-end clipping: rd(Feb 28, Jan 31) = 1 month but the reverse = 0 months/28 d — so I canonicalize direction and keep direction in the existing sign).
- 1 completed month → describe("month", sign) ("a month ago" / "in a month"), now for every month length.
- ≥ 2 → describe("months", sign * n) — fixes both the 59-day "2-month shift → 'a month'" and the 74-day over-count.
- 0 → weeks count, so genuine sub-month spans (e.g. Mar 1 → Mar 30) still say "4 weeks" and aren't misbucketed.
